      "id": 1,
      "text": "Tollette sprang from freed slaves after the Civil War, with Stephen Tollette buying farmland and naming the town after his sons Stephen and Sanford.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tollette_arkansas",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Tollette traces its roots to early 19th‑century settlement after the Caddo people were removed to Indian Territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tollette_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Loblolly pine forests dominate Howard County, and Tollette’s history is tied to logging and pulpwood production.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tollette_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cotton once thrived in Tollette, but the industry collapsed in the early 1900s, with the last bale ginned in 1971.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tollette_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1891, Tollette secured its first post office, choosing the name Tollette after the founder’s family.",
